> Added in prompt v3 from R003 evidence; v3 takes effect at the R004 run boundary, and R003 is assessed under v2:
> 
> - Controller acceptance numbers for a repair come from an independent computation on the raw data, never from the artifact being corrected. Record the raw-data source (hashes or snapshot) and the recount script, keep the original target and the reason for any revision on record, and let a worker that cannot match the numbers explain the difference with examples; recheck from raw data before deciding who is right. Independent Howl review and acceptance still decide the repair; if the sources still disagree, pause under section 17. (Trigger: 2026-10-10T03:54:13Z.)
> - Before a design's labels are frozen, run a structure-only check (no comparison of outcomes with predictions) of each label rule against how the source encodes the case, using the largest dataset already retrieved under existing authority, and record the counts per encoding; if no such data exists, record the rule as untested. A label found wrong after freezing is changed only by a dated user decision under section 17, which also decides whether the analysis stays confirmatory; the old definition's result is still reported. If the defect is found after a model fit, pause, and keep the earlier results reported unchanged. (Trigger: 2026-10-10T06:16:06Z.)
> - Work that needs more wall time than a HowlPlane worker budget allows, such as a long rate-limited data pull, is split: a session builds and tests the tool with a small live proof, which counts against the request budget; the controller runs the accepted tool (recording its commit) through its public interface as ordinary product use, within the user's written authorization and remaining request limits, resumably and with logged requests, and pauses under section 17 on failures or if the pull would exceed the authorization; a later session processes the result. (Trigger: 2026-10-10T04:00:14Z, NOTE-009.)
> - Journal heading times come from `date -u` at the moment of writing, never from estimates. A late entry states both the write time and the event time; corrections are appended, never edited. (Trigger: 2026-10-09T19:40Z.)
